    dmo:prevalenceShare "0.01"^^xsd:decimal ;
    dmo:typicalOnset "AnyAge" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "• Specific types of diabetes due to other causes. This may include women with monogenic diabetes or maturity-onset diabetes of the young (MODY), cystic fibrosis, pancreatitis related diabetes and chemical or drug induced diabetes." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/diabetesType/type-1-diabetes> a dmo:DiabetesType ;
    rdfs:label "Type 1 Diabetes" ;
    dmo:definition "different types of diabetes (type 1, type 2 or gestational) would require different approaches depending upon the risk factors, antenatal treatment (diet, metformin, insulin), risk of hypoglycaemia, risk of anaesthesia and the presence of obstetric complications." ;
    dmo:diabetesTypeCode "type-1-diabetes" ;
    dmo:insulinRequired "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:mondoCode "MONDO:0005147" ;
    dmo:name "Type 1 Diabetes" ;
    dmo:prevalenceShare "0.1"^^xsd:decimal ;
    dmo:typicalOnset "Childhood" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "different types of diabetes (type 1, type 2 or gestational) would require different approaches depending upon the risk factors, antenatal treatment (diet, metformin, insulin), risk of hypoglycaemia, risk of anaesthesia and the presence of obstetric complications." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Women with type 1 diabetes are at increased risk of severe hypoglycaemia especially when breastfeeding and should have additional carbohydrate with meal or as a snack available during or before breastfeeds." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Most women will self-manage their insulin pump doses, often with assistance from their birth partner. They will use correction boluses and/or basal rate changes to maintain target glucose levels following steroid administration and before and during birth" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "NICE recommends that babies of mothers with all types of diabetes should be monitored for neonatal hypoglycaemia for at least 24 hours post-delivery (1)." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Women with type 1 diabetes should be screened for post-partum thyroiditis with a TSH at 3 and 6 months postpartum" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "The incidence of pre-eclampsia in women with type 1 diabetes is 10-15%." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "The main types of diabetes likely to be encountered on the obstetric wards are as follows (34):\n• Type 1 diabetes" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Neonatal hypoglycaemia results from excessive fetal insulin production as a consequence of the sudden cessation of maternal-fetal glucose transfer after birth." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "The National Pregnancy in Diabetes audit (NPID) of 17,375 women with type 1 and type 2 diabetes showed that the prevalence of delivery before 37 weeks is approximately 45% in type 1 and 25% in type 2 diabetes." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "The average duration of hyperglycaemia was 3 days with almost all women (>90%) experiencing hyperglycaemia >7.0 mmol/L, and over 50% with hyperglycaemia >10.0 mmol/L. Rates of maternal hypoglycaemia before and after corticosteroid administration were high (49% and 58% of mothers with type 1 diabetes); further emphasising the importance of weighing up both the maternal and neonatal consequences (49)." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Percentage of women with type 1 and type 2 diabetes on VRIII during delivery whose insulin dose was reduced by 50% after the delivery of placenta and changed to subcutaneous regimen appropriately 100%" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Severe neonatal hypoglycaemia and intrapartum glycaemic control in pregnancies complicated by type 1, type 2 and gestational diabetes." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/diabetesType/type-1-dm> a dmo:DiabetesType ;
    rdfs:label "Type 1 DM" ;
    dmo:definition "Type 1 DM and INSULIN TREATED TYPE 2 DM" ;
    dmo:diabetesTypeCode "type-1-dm" ;
    dmo:insulinRequired "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:mondoCode "MONDO:0005147" ;
    dmo:name "Type 1 DM" ;
    dmo:prevalenceShare "0.1"^^xsd:decimal ;
    dmo:typicalOnset "Childhood" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "TYPE 1 DM and INSULIN TREATED TYPE 2 DM" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Patients with type 1 DM on insulin pumps should be referred to the Diabetes Specialist Team" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/diabetesType/type-2-diabetes> a dmo:DiabetesType ;
    rdfs:label "Type 2 Diabetes" ;
    dmo:definition "different types of diabetes (type 1, type 2 or gestational) would require different approaches depending upon the risk factors, antenatal treatment (diet, metformin, insulin), risk of hypoglycaemia, risk of anaesthesia and the presence of obstetric complications." ;
    dmo:diabetesTypeCode "type-2-diabetes" ;
    dmo:insulinRequired "false"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:mondoCode "MONDO:0005148" ;
    dmo:name "Type 2 Diabetes" ;
    dmo:prevalenceShare "0.85"^^xsd:decimal ;
    dmo:typicalOnset "Adulthood" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "different types of diabetes (type 1, type 2 or gestational) would require different approaches depending upon the risk factors, antenatal treatment (diet, metformin, insulin), risk of hypoglycaemia, risk of anaesthesia and the presence of obstetric complications." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Women with gestational diabetes have a ten times increased risk of developing type 2 diabetes within 5 years" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Neonatal hypoglycaemia results from excessive fetal insulin production as a consequence of the sudden cessation of maternal-fetal glucose transfer after birth." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "The National Pregnancy in Diabetes audit (NPID) of 17,375 women with type 1 and type 2 diabetes showed that the prevalence of delivery before 37 weeks is approximately 45% in type 1 and 25% in type 2 diabetes." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "The average duration of hyperglycaemia was 3 days with almost all women (>90%) experiencing hyperglycaemia >7.0 mmol/L, and over 50% with hyperglycaemia >10.0 mmol/L. Rates of maternal hypoglycaemia before and after corticosteroid administration were high (49% and 58% of mothers with type 1 diabetes); further emphasising the importance of weighing up both the maternal and neonatal consequences (49)." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Percentage of women with type 1 and type 2 diabetes on VRIII during delivery whose insulin dose was reduced by 50% after the delivery of placenta and changed to subcutaneous regimen appropriately 100%" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Progression to type 2 diabetes in women with a known history of gestational diabetes: systematic review and meta-analysis." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Severe neonatal hypoglycaemia and intrapartum glycaemic control in pregnancies complicated by type 1, type 2 and gestational diabetes." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/diabetesType/type-2-dm> a dmo:DiabetesType ;
    rdfs:label "Type 2 DM" ;
    dmo:definition "Patients with type 1 DM on insulin pumps should be referred to the Diabetes Specialist Team" ;
    dmo:diabetesTypeCode "type-2-dm" ;
    dmo:insulinRequired "false"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:mondoCode "MONDO:0005148" ;
    dmo:name "Type 2 DM" ;
    dmo:prevalenceShare "0.9"^^xsd:decimal ;
    dmo:typicalOnset "Adulthood" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Patients with type 1 DM on insulin pumps should be referred to the Diabetes Specialist Team" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/actrapid> a dmo:DrugClass ;
    rdfs:label "Actrapid®" ;
    dmo:adminRoute "Subcutaneous" ;
    dmo:atcCode "A10AB01" ;
    dmo:drugClassCode "actrapid" ;
    dmo:hypoglycemiaRisk "High" ;
    dmo:mechanism "Short-acting insulin used for mealtime glucose control" ;
    dmo:name "Actrapid®" ;
    dmo:weightEffect "Gain" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "(50 units Actrapid® or Humulin® S insulin in 49.5 mL 0.9% NaCl via syringe driver)" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/basal-insulin> a dmo:DrugClass ;
    rdfs:label "Basal Insulin" ;
    dmo:drugClassCode "basal-insulin" ;
    dmo:hypoglycemiaRisk "Low" ;
    dmo:mechanism "Provides a continuous background level of insulin to control fasting blood glucose" ;
    dmo:name "Basal Insulin" ;
    dmo:weightEffect "Neutral" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Continue with the basal insulin i.e. Glargine (Lantus®, Abasaglar®, Toujeo®), Detemir (Levemir®) or Degludec (Tresiba®) but discontinue short acting insulin" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/continuous-subcutaneous-insulin-infusion> a dmo:DrugClass ;
    rdfs:label "Continuous Subcutaneous Insulin Infusion" ;
    dmo:adminRoute "Subcutaneous" ;
    dmo:drugClassCode "continuous-subcutaneous-insulin-infusion" ;
    dmo:hypoglycemiaRisk "High" ;
    dmo:mechanism "Continuous delivery of insulin under the skin, often used in conjunction with continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) for real-time glucose management" ;
    dmo:name "Continuous Subcutaneous Insulin Infusion" ;
    dmo:weightEffect "Neutral" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Following the December 2020 NICE guideline update pregnant women with type 1 diabetes are increasingly using Continuous Glucose Monitoring (CGM) alone or in conjunction with Continuous Subcutaneous Insulin Infusion (CSII) or hybrid closed-loop systems (4; 34-36). These are usually continued to support diabetes self-management during antenatal hospital admissions, including during and after antenatal steroids administration and before and during birth (See Table 3)." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/continuous-subcutaneous-insulin-infusion-csii> a dmo:DrugClass ;
    rdfs:label "Continuous Subcutaneous Insulin Infusion (CSII)" ;
    dmo:adminRoute "Subcutaneous" ;
    dmo:drugClassCode "continuous-subcutaneous-insulin-infusion-csii" ;
    dmo:hypoglycemiaRisk "High" ;
    dmo:mechanism "CSII delivers insulin continuously through a pump, providing basal insulin and allowing for bolus doses at meal times, thereby mimicking physiological insulin secretion." ;
    dmo:name "Continuous Subcutaneous Insulin Infusion (CSII)" ;
    dmo:weightEffect "Neutral" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Observational data in 161 pregnant women with type 1 diabetes using insulin pumps or VRIII demonstrated that women who continued to self-manage their diabetes using CSII achieved lower glucose levels during delivery than those who were switched to IV insulin (38). The authors noted high rates of maternal hypoglycaemia associated with tight intrapartum glucose targets." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/fast-acting-insulin> a dmo:DrugClass ;
    rdfs:label "Fast-Acting Insulin" ;
    dmo:drugClassCode "fast-acting-insulin" ;
    dmo:hypoglycemiaRisk "High" ;
    dmo:mechanism "Rapidly lowers blood glucose by promoting glucose uptake in muscle and fat tissues and inhibiting hepatic glucose production" ;
    dmo:name "Fast-Acting Insulin" ;
    dmo:weightEffect "Neutral" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Continue with the basal insulin i.e. Glargine (Lantus®, Abasaglar®, Toujeo®), Detemir (Levemir®) or Degludec (Tresiba®) but discontinue short acting insulin" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/fixed-rate-intravenous-insulin-infusion> a dmo:DrugClass ;
    rdfs:label "Fixed Rate Intravenous Insulin Infusion" ;
    dmo:adminRoute "Intravenous" ;
    dmo:atcCode "A10BA02" ;
    dmo:drugClassCode "fixed-rate-intravenous-insulin-infusion" ;
    dmo:hypoglycemiaRisk "High" ;
    dmo:mechanism "Insulin delivered continuously via IV infusion at a fixed rate, used in the management of diabetic ketoacidosis." ;
    dmo:name "Fixed Rate Intravenous Insulin Infusion" ;
    dmo:weightEffect "Neutral" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "This protocol is based on national guidance (27) which uses a fixed rate of insulin infusion (FRIII) and a variable amount of intravenous glucose to prevent hypoglycaemia." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/human-soluble-insulin> a dmo:DrugClass ;
    rdfs:label "Human soluble insulin" ;
    dmo:adminRoute "Subcutaneous" ;
    dmo:atcCode "A10AE01" ;
    dmo:drugClassCode "human-soluble-insulin" ;
    dmo:hypoglycemiaRisk "High" ;
    dmo:mechanism "Acts by binding to insulin receptors on target cells, promoting glucose uptake and inhibiting glucose production in the liver." ;
    dmo:name "Human soluble insulin" ;
    dmo:weightEffect "Neutral" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Fluids, particularly dextrose containing fluids, may have to be restricted in patients who are at risk of or already have hyponatraemia. In some cases insulin without substrate fluids may have to be used (e.g. difficult i.v. access, fluid overload states like toxaemia, hyponatraemia or risk of hyponatraemia)." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/humulin-s> a dmo:DrugClass ;
    rdfs:label "Humulin® S" ;
    dmo:adminRoute "Subcutaneous" ;
    dmo:atcCode "A10AB01" ;
    dmo:drugClassCode "humulin-s" ;
    dmo:hypoglycemiaRisk "High" ;
    dmo:mechanism "Short-acting insulin used for mealtime glucose control" ;
    dmo:name "Humulin® S" ;
    dmo:weightEffect "Gain" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "(50 units Actrapid® or Humulin® S insulin in 49.5 mL 0.9% NaCl via syringe driver)" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/insulin> a dmo:DrugClass ;
    rdfs:label "Insulin" ;
    dmo:adminRoute "Subcutaneous" ;
    dmo:atcCode "A10A" ;
    dmo:drugClassCode "insulin" ;
    dmo:hypoglycemiaRisk "High" ;
    dmo:mechanism "Insulin is a hormone that lowers blood glucose levels by promoting glucose uptake into cells and inhibiting glucose production in the liver." ;
    dmo:name "Insulin" ;
    dmo:weightEffect "Neutral" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "There is broad consensus that striving for target glucose levels safely is desirable in all pregnant women with diabetes when admitted to maternity units." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Women with pre-existing type 2 diabetes who are breastfeeding can take metformin after birth, but should avoid other oral glucose lowering treatments" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Manufacturers liability concerns of pump failure near diathermy sites" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Insulin (s.c.) may need to be started in women managed by diet or metformin and an increase in s.c. insulin dose typically by 50% is needed in those who are already on insulin." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "If the woman is using an insulin pump discontinue the insulin pump and start intravenous insulin infusion at a fixed rate." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Consequently, this updated JBDS guideline recommends administering 0.9% saline with 5% dextrose and 0.15% potassium chloride alongside a VRIII. This is in accordance with NICE guidance on prevention of inpatient hyponatraemia in hospitalised children and young people. To avoid fluid overload, the rate of this fluid should not exceed 50 mL/h." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "NICE therefore recommends women with insulin-treated diabetes are given additional insulin according to an agreed protocol and are monitored closely (1)." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "JBDS recommends adjusting insulin dose (MDI or CSII) by 50-80% to maintain glycaemic control with regular glucose monitoring. If the glucose levels are consistently higher than target levels then VRIII can be used (Appendix 2 or Appendix 4)" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "ALWAYS continue subcutaneous intermediate* or basal insulin** *Intermediate: Insulatard, Humulin I, Insuman basal **Basal: Lantus and Toujeo (Glargine), Levemir (Detemir)" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/insulin-detemir> a dmo:DrugClass ;
    rdfs:label "Insulin Detemir" ;
    dmo:adminRoute "Subcutaneous" ;
    dmo:atcCode "A10AE02" ;
    dmo:drugClassCode "insulin-detemir" ;
    dmo:hypoglycemiaRisk "High" ;
    dmo:mechanism "Long-acting insulin analog that provides basal insulin coverage" ;
    dmo:name "Insulin Detemir" ;
    dmo:weightEffect "Gain" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Basal insulin should be continued in women using insulin glargine (Lantus®, Toujeo®, Semglee®, Abasaglar®), Detemir (Levemir®), NPH insulin (Insulatard®), Insuman® Basal or Humulin® I or other basal insulins" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/insulin-glargine> a dmo:DrugClass ;
    rdfs:label "Insulin Glargine" ;
    dmo:adminRoute "Subcutaneous" ;
    dmo:atcCode "A10AE01" ;
    dmo:drugClassCode "insulin-glargine" ;
    dmo:hypoglycemiaRisk "High" ;
    dmo:mechanism "Long-acting insulin analog that provides basal insulin coverage" ;
    dmo:name "Insulin Glargine" ;
    dmo:weightEffect "Gain" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Basal insulin should be continued in women using insulin glargine (Lantus®, Toujeo®, Semglee®, Abasaglar®)" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/insulin-humulin-i> a dmo:DrugClass ;
    rdfs:label "Insulin Humulin I" ;
    dmo:adminRoute "Subcutaneous" ;
    dmo:atcCode "A10AD01" ;
    dmo:drugClassCode "insulin-humulin-i" ;
    dmo:hypoglycemiaRisk "High" ;
    dmo:mechanism "Intermediate-acting insulin providing basal insulin coverage" ;
    dmo:name "Insulin Humulin I" ;
    dmo:weightEffect "Gain" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Basal insulin should be continued in women using insulin glargine (Lantus®, Toujeo®, Semglee®, Abasaglar®), Detemir (Levemir®), NPH insulin (Insulatard®), Insuman® Basal or Humulin® I or other basal insulins" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/insulin-infusions> a dmo:DrugClass ;
    rdfs:label "Insulin Infusions" ;
    dmo:adminRoute "Intravenous" ;
    dmo:drugClassCode "insulin-infusions" ;
    dmo:hypoglycemiaRisk "High" ;
    dmo:mechanism "Delivers insulin directly into the bloodstream to lower blood glucose levels" ;
    dmo:name "Insulin Infusions" ;
    dmo:weightEffect "Neutral" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Up to one in two women treated with intensive insulin infusions during labour experience clinically significant hypoglycaemia (38)." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/insulin-insuman-basal> a dmo:DrugClass ;
    rdfs:label "Insulin Insuman Basal" ;
    dmo:adminRoute "Subcutaneous" ;
    dmo:atcCode "A10AD01" ;
    dmo:drugClassCode "insulin-insuman-basal" ;
    dmo:hypoglycemiaRisk "High" ;
    dmo:mechanism "Intermediate-acting insulin providing basal insulin coverage" ;
    dmo:name "Insulin Insuman Basal" ;
    dmo:weightEffect "Gain" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Basal insulin should be continued in women using insulin glargine (Lantus®, Toujeo®, Semglee®, Abasaglar®), Detemir (Levemir®), NPH insulin (Insulatard®), Insuman® Basal or Humulin® I or other basal insulins" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/intravenous-dextrose-and-insulin-infusion> a dmo:DrugClass ;
    rdfs:label "Intravenous Dextrose and Insulin Infusion" ;
    dmo:adminRoute "Intravenous" ;
    dmo:drugClassCode "intravenous-dextrose-and-insulin-infusion" ;
    dmo:hypoglycemiaRisk "High" ;
    dmo:mechanism "Intravenous infusion of dextrose and insulin to manage hyperglycaemia during labour and birth." ;
    dmo:name "Intravenous Dextrose and Insulin Infusion" ;
    dmo:weightEffect "Neutral" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Intravenous dextrose and insulin infusion should be considered for women with type 1 diabetes from the onset of established labour. Use intravenous dextrose and insulin infusion during labour and birth for women with diabetes whose capillary plasma glucose is not maintained between 4.0 and 7.0 mmol/L." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/metformin> a dmo:DrugClass ;
    rdfs:label "Metformin" ;
    dmo:adminRoute "Oral" ;
    dmo:atcCode "A10BA01" ;
    dmo:drugClassCode "metformin" ;
    dmo:hypoglycemiaRisk "Low" ;
    dmo:mechanism "Metformin reduces hepatic glucose production and improves insulin sensitivity." ;
    dmo:name "Metformin" ;
    dmo:weightEffect "Neutral" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Women with pre-existing type 2 diabetes who are breastfeeding can take metformin after birth, but should avoid other oral glucose lowering treatments" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Prandial insulin (and metformin if taken) should be stopped if VRIII is started" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Because metformin does not cause hypoglycaemia, a target glucose range of 4.0 - 10.0 mmol/L is acceptable." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Insulin (s.c.) may need to be started in women managed by diet or metformin and an increase in s.c. insulin dose typically by 50% is needed in those who are already on insulin." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/nph-insulin> a dmo:DrugClass ;
    rdfs:label "NPH Insulin" ;
    dmo:adminRoute "Subcutaneous" ;
    dmo:atcCode "A10AD01" ;
    dmo:drugClassCode "nph-insulin" ;
    dmo:hypoglycemiaRisk "High" ;
    dmo:mechanism "Intermediate-acting insulin providing basal insulin coverage" ;
    dmo:name "NPH Insulin" ;
    dmo:weightEffect "Gain" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Basal insulin should be continued in women using insulin glargine (Lantus®, Toujeo®, Semglee®, Abasaglar®), Detemir (Levemir®), NPH insulin (Insulatard®), Insuman® Basal or Humulin® I or other basal insulins" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/subcutaneous-insulin> a dmo:DrugClass ;
    rdfs:label "Subcutaneous Insulin" ;
    dmo:adminRoute "Subcutaneous" ;
    dmo:drugClassCode "subcutaneous-insulin" ;
    dmo:hypoglycemiaRisk "High" ;
    dmo:mechanism "Insulin administered under the skin to lower blood glucose levels." ;
    dmo:name "Subcutaneous Insulin" ;
    dmo:weightEffect "Neutral" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "If the BG levels are higher than target on two consecutive occasions, VRIII can be used to manage hyperglycaemia (Appendix 2 for NICE recommended targets and Appendix 4 for liberal targets)" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/variable-rate-intravenous-insulin-infusion> a dmo:DrugClass ;
    rdfs:label "Variable Rate Intravenous Insulin Infusion" ;
    dmo:adminRoute "Intravenous" ;
    dmo:drugClassCode "variable-rate-intravenous-insulin-infusion" ;
    dmo:hypoglycemiaRisk "High" ;
    dmo:mechanism "Insulin infusion adjusted according to hourly capillary blood glucose levels to manage hyperglycaemia" ;
    dmo:name "Variable Rate Intravenous Insulin Infusion" ;
    dmo:weightEffect "Neutral" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "However, the safety and efficacy of VRIII use following antenatal steroids administration and before or during birth in busy obstetric ward/delivery unit settings are uncertain (See Table 2)." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/drugClass/vriii> a dmo:DrugClass ;
    rdfs:label "VRIII" ;
    dmo:adminRoute "Intravenous" ;
    dmo:atcCode "A10AE01" ;
    dmo:drugClassCode "vriii" ;
    dmo:hypoglycemiaRisk "High" ;
    dmo:mechanism "Insulin administered intravenously to maintain glycaemic control during labour" ;
    dmo:name "VRIII" ;
    dmo:weightEffect "Neutral" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "VRIII without substrate fluids may be required in some cases (e.g. fluid overload, hyponatraemia and pre-eclampsia). Pure dextrose containing fluids should be avoided due to risk of hyponatraemia" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Consequently, this updated JBDS guideline recommends administering 0.9% saline with 5% dextrose and 0.15% potassium chloride alongside a VRIII. This is in accordance with NICE guidance on prevention of inpatient hyponatraemia in hospitalised children and young people. To avoid fluid overload, the rate of this fluid should not exceed 50 mL/h." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/bicarbonate> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Bicarbonate" ;
    dmo:definition "Bicarbonate <15 mmol/L is a criterion for acidosis in DKA diagnosis; note that bicarbonate is reduced in pregnancy." ;
    dmo:labTestCode "bicarbonate" ;
    dmo:name "Bicarbonate" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "mmol/L" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "3. Acidosis: blood gas pH <7.3 and/or bicarbonate <15 mmol/L (N.B. bicarbonate is reduced in pregnancy). Use venous blood gases." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/blood-glucose> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Blood Glucose" ;
    dmo:definition "Blood glucose levels are monitored hourly in established labour, after artificial rupture of membrane or on admission for caesarean section. Two consecutive blood glucose levels above the target range (7.0 or 8.0 mmol/L) should trigger consideration of VRIII." ;
    dmo:fastingRequired "false"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:labTestCode "blood-glucose" ;
    dmo:name "Blood Glucose" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "mmol/L" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "The peripartum diabetes management plan includes hourly glucose monitoring using CGM or capillary glucose measurement during established labour or after artificial rupture of membrane or on admission for caesarean section" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "ALL women with diabetes should have Blood Glucose (BG) or intermittent or real time continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) testing hourly in established labour, after ARM or on admission for elective C-Section" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Finger prick BG Levels (mmol/L) For most women" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Check BG every hour whilst on VRIII\nMove to the higher algorithm if the BG is above target and is not dropping\n Move to the lower algorithm if BG falls below 5.0 mmol/L or is dropping too fast" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "There was substantial individual variation and it was difficult to achieve and maintain tight glucose control (4.0 - 7.0 mmol/L) in both the study and control groups." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "There is no high quality research evidence to guide us about the optimal target glucose levels during labour, delivery and birth." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "o ALL women with diabetes should have Blood Glucose (BG) or intermittent or real time continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) testing hourly in established labour, after ARM or on admission for elective C-Section" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "ALL women on VRIII should have Blood Glucose (BG) testing hourly for the management of steroid hyperglycaemia during pregnancy" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Check BG hourly for further 24 hours after the last dose of steroid OR as per advice form the Diabetes Team" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/blood-ketones> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Blood Ketones" ;
    dmo:definition "Blood ketones should be checked if ketoacidosis is suspected during ongoing use of VRIII (>6 hours)." ;
    dmo:fastingRequired "false"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:labTestCode "blood-ketones" ;
    dmo:name "Blood Ketones" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "mmol/L" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "During ongoing use of VRIII (>6 hours), serum electrolytes should be checked 4 – 6 hourly to maintain electrolyte balance. If ketoacidosis is suspected, blood ketones should be checked" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "2. Ketosis: urinary ketones >++ or blood ketones >3.0 mmol/L (high risk 1.5 mmol/L)" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Check BG hourly for further 24 hours after the last dose of steroid OR as per advice form the Diabetes Team" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/capillary-blood-glucose> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Capillary Blood Glucose" ;
    dmo:definition "Capillary blood glucose levels are used to monitor glucose levels during labour, after delivery, and when administering steroids in pregnancy." ;
    dmo:labTestCode "capillary-blood-glucose" ;
    dmo:name "Capillary Blood Glucose" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "mmol/L" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "All women with diabetes of any type should have hourly blood glucose (capillary, Flash or CGM) monitoring in established labour or from the morning of elective caesarean section." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/capillary-glucose> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Capillary Glucose" ;
    dmo:definition "Capillary glucose tests may be required during labour and delivery for women using intermittent or real time continuous glucose monitoring (CGM). CGM measures interstitial glucose levels, and changes may lag 5 - 10 minutes behind capillary blood glucose measurements." ;
    dmo:fastingRequired "false"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:labTestCode "capillary-glucose" ;
    dmo:name "Capillary Glucose" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "mmol/L" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Women using intermittent or real time continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) should be advised that additional capillary glucose tests may be required during labour and delivery. CGM measures interstitial glucose levels, and changes may lag 5 - 10 minutes behind capillary blood glucose measurements." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Measure capillary glucose hourly" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Monitor blood glucose (BG) and capillary ketones (if available) hourly, venous bicarbonate and potassium at 1 hour, 2 hours, 4 hours and then depending upon the need, serum electrolytes 4 hourly (27)" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Start VRIII and Fluids if two consecutive BG/CGM > target and continue for up to 12 hours after the last dose of steroid" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/capillary-glucose-monitoring> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Capillary Glucose Monitoring" ;
    dmo:labTestCode "capillary-glucose-monitoring" ;
    dmo:name "Capillary Glucose Monitoring" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "mmol/L" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "During the CONCEPTT trial in type 1 diabetes pregnancy, rates of clinically relevant neonatal hypoglycaemia were 15% in women using CGM compared to 28% with women using capillary glucose monitoring (4)." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/capillary-ketones> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Capillary Ketones" ;
    dmo:definition "Capillary ketones are monitored hourly if available during the management of DKA in pregnant women." ;
    dmo:fastingRequired "false"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:labTestCode "capillary-ketones" ;
    dmo:name "Capillary Ketones" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Monitor blood glucose (BG) and capillary ketones (if available) hourly, venous bicarbonate and potassium at 1 hour, 2 hours, 4 hours and then depending upon the need, serum electrolytes 4 hourly (27)" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/capillary-plasma-glucose> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Capillary Plasma Glucose" ;
    dmo:definition "Plasma glucose measured from capillary blood, used to monitor glucose levels during labour and birth." ;
    dmo:labTestCode "capillary-plasma-glucose" ;
    dmo:name "Capillary Plasma Glucose" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "mmol/L" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Use intravenous dextrose and insulin infusion during labour and birth for women with diabetes whose capillary plasma glucose is not maintained between 4.0 and 7.0 mmol/L." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/continuous-glucose-monitoring> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Continuous Glucose Monitoring" ;
    dmo:definition "Continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) is used to monitor glucose levels during labour, delivery, and postpartum, especially in women using insulin pump therapy or automated insulin delivery systems." ;
    dmo:labTestCode "continuous-glucose-monitoring" ;
    dmo:name "Continuous Glucose Monitoring" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "mmol/L" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "All women with diabetes of any type should have hourly blood glucose (capillary, Flash or CGM) monitoring in established labour or from the morning of elective caesarean section." ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "ALL women with diabetes should have Blood Glucose (BG) or intermittent or real time continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) testing hourly in established labour, after ARM or on admission for elective C-Section" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "During the CONCEPTT trial in type 1 diabetes pregnancy, rates of clinically relevant neonatal hypoglycaemia were 15% in women using CGM compared to 28% with women using capillary glucose monitoring (4)." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/fasting-glucose> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Fasting Glucose" ;
    dmo:definition "A diagnostic test for glucose levels after an overnight fast, used to assess glucose metabolism and diagnose diabetes or impaired fasting glucose." ;
    dmo:fastingRequired "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:labTestCode "fasting-glucose" ;
    dmo:name "Fasting Glucose" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "mmol/L" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Percentage of pregnant women with diabetes who receive CG as per JBDS or an agreed and audited trust guideline after delivery 100%" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/fasting-plasma-glucose> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Fasting Plasma Glucose" ;
    dmo:definition "Fasting plasma glucose should be done at 6-13 weeks after delivery to diagnose diabetes post-partum." ;
    dmo:fastingRequired "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:labTestCode "fasting-plasma-glucose" ;
    dmo:loincCode "15577-7" ;
    dmo:name "Fasting Plasma Glucose" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "mmol-per-L" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "For women with GDM, fasting plasma glucose should be done at 6-13 weeks after delivery to diagnose diabetes post-partum." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/flash-glucose-monitoring> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Flash Glucose Monitoring" ;
    dmo:definition "Flash glucose monitoring is used as an alternative to capillary blood glucose for monitoring glucose levels during labour and postpartum." ;
    dmo:labTestCode "flash-glucose-monitoring" ;
    dmo:name "Flash Glucose Monitoring" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "mmol/L" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "All women with diabetes of any type should have hourly blood glucose (capillary, Flash or CGM) monitoring in established labour or from the morning of elective caesarean section." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/hba1c> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "HbA1c" ;
    dmo:definition "HbA1c after 13 weeks is an alternative if the fasting plasma glucose is not done before 13 weeks post-partum. Annual HbA1c measurement is required during further follow up in women who have a negative post-natal test." ;
    dmo:fastingRequired "false"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:labTestCode "hba1c" ;
    dmo:loincCode "4548-4" ;
    dmo:name "HbA1c" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "mmol-per-mol" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "HbA1c after 13 weeks is an alternative if the fasting plasma glucose is not done before 13 weeks post-partum (1). Annual HbA1c measurement is required during further follow up in women who have a negative post-natal test (1)" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/high-ketones> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "High Ketones" ;
    dmo:definition "high ketones (>1.5 mmol/L)" ;
    dmo:labTestCode "high-ketones" ;
    dmo:name "High Ketones" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "mmol/L" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "or has urinary ketones ++ or more, or high ketones (>1.5 mmol/L), then VRIII (in hyperglycaemia) or FRIII (in ketoacidosis) should be immediately commenced" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/intrapartum-glycaemia> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Intrapartum Glycaemia" ;
    dmo:labTestCode "intrapartum-glycaemia" ;
    dmo:name "Intrapartum Glycaemia" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "mmol/L" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "A subsequent secondary analysis found no difference in intrapartum glycaemia between mothers of neonates with and without hypoglycaemia (39)." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/ketones> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Ketones" ;
    dmo:definition "Ketones are toxic to the fetus and are a key feature in diagnosing diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA). DKA may present with ketosis even with only modest elevation of glucose levels ('euglycaemic DKA')." ;
    dmo:fastingRequired "false"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:labTestCode "ketones" ;
    dmo:name "Ketones" ;
    dmo:specimen "Urine" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Ketones are toxic to the fetus, and there were 11 still births and 1 neonatal death. These data showed that the most common precipitants were infection, vomiting, steroid treatment or medication errors (26)." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/maternal-hyperglycaemia-during-labour> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Maternal Hyperglycaemia During Labour" ;
    dmo:labTestCode "maternal-hyperglycaemia-during-labour" ;
    dmo:name "Maternal Hyperglycaemia During Labour" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "mmol/L" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Recent data contrast with previous studies suggesting that maternal hyperglycaemia during labour was associated with an increased risk of neonatal hypoglycaemia (37)." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/neonatal-hypoglycaemia> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Neonatal Hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:labTestCode "neonatal-hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:name "Neonatal Hypoglycaemia" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "mmol/L" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Neonatal hypoglycaemia results from excessive fetal insulin production as a consequence of the sudden cessation of maternal-fetal glucose transfer after birth." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/ogtt> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "OGTT" ;
    dmo:definition "Oral Glucose Tolerance Test, a diagnostic test used to assess glucose tolerance and diagnose gestational diabetes mellitus after a fasting period and ingestion of a glucose load." ;
    dmo:fastingRequired "true"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:labTestCode "ogtt" ;
    dmo:name "OGTT" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "mmol/L" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Percentage of pregnant women with diabetes who receive CG as per JBDS or an agreed and audited trust guideline after delivery 100%" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/potassium> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Potassium" ;
    dmo:definition "Potassium is monitored at 1 hour, 2 hours, 4 hours and then depending upon the need during DKA management. Aim for keeping K+ between 4.0 and 5.5 mmol/L." ;
    dmo:fastingRequired "false"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:labTestCode "potassium" ;
    dmo:name "Potassium" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "mmol/L" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Monitor blood glucose (BG) and capillary ketones (if available) hourly, venous bicarbonate and potassium at 1 hour, 2 hours, 4 hours and then depending upon the need, serum electrolytes 4 hourly (27)" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/serum-electrolytes> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Serum Electrolytes" ;
    dmo:definition "Serum electrolytes are monitored 4 hourly during DKA management." ;
    dmo:fastingRequired "false"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:labTestCode "serum-electrolytes" ;
    dmo:name "Serum Electrolytes" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Monitor blood glucose (BG) and capillary ketones (if available) hourly, venous bicarbonate and potassium at 1 hour, 2 hours, 4 hours and then depending upon the need, serum electrolytes 4 hourly (27)" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/tsh> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "TSH" ;
    dmo:definition "Women with type 1 diabetes should be screened for post-partum thyroiditis with a TSH at 3 and 6 months postpartum." ;
    dmo:fastingRequired "false"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:labTestCode "tsh" ;
    dmo:loincCode "2401-1" ;
    dmo:name "TSH" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "mIU-per-L" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Women with type 1 diabetes should be screened for post-partum thyroiditis with a TSH at 3 and 6 months postpartum (24)" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/urinary-ketones> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Urinary Ketones" ;
    dmo:definition "urinary ketones ++ or more" ;
    dmo:labTestCode "urinary-ketones" ;
    dmo:name "Urinary Ketones" ;
    dmo:specimen "Urine" ;
    dmo:unitOfMeasure "++ or more" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "or has urinary ketones ++ or more, or high ketones (>1.5 mmol/L), then VRIII (in hyperglycaemia) or FRIII (in ketoacidosis) should be immediately commenced" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "2. Ketosis: urinary ketones >++ or blood ketones >3.0 mmol/L (high risk 1.5 mmol/L)" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/urine-ketones> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Urine Ketones" ;
    dmo:definition "Urine ketones are monitored in patients with type 1 DM on insulin pumps" ;
    dmo:fastingRequired "false"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:labTestCode "urine-ketones" ;
    dmo:name "Urine Ketones" ;
    dmo:specimen "Urine" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Patients with type 1 DM on insulin pumps should be referred to the Diabetes Specialist Team" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/venous-bicarbonate> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Venous Bicarbonate" ;
    dmo:definition "Venous bicarbonate is monitored at 1 hour, 2 hours, 4 hours and then depending upon the need during DKA management." ;
    dmo:fastingRequired "false"^^xsd:boolean ;
    dmo:labTestCode "venous-bicarbonate" ;
    dmo:name "Venous Bicarbonate" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Monitor blood glucose (BG) and capillary ketones (if available) hourly, venous bicarbonate and potassium at 1 hour, 2 hours, 4 hours and then depending upon the need, serum electrolytes 4 hourly (27)" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/labTest/venous-blood-gas-ph> a dmo:LabTest ;
    rdfs:label "Venous Blood Gas pH" ;
    dmo:definition "Blood gas pH <7.3 is a criterion for acidosis in DKA diagnosis; use venous blood gases in pregnancy." ;
    dmo:labTestCode "venous-blood-gas-ph" ;
    dmo:name "Venous Blood Gas pH" ;
    dmo:specimen "Blood" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "3. Acidosis: blood gas pH <7.3 and/or bicarbonate <15 mmol/L (N.B. bicarbonate is reduced in pregnancy). Use venous blood gases." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/lifestyleIntervention/healthy-eating> a dmo:LifestyleIntervention ;
    rdfs:label "Healthy eating" ;
    dmo:interventionCode "healthy-eating" ;
    dmo:interventionType "Nutrition" ;
    dmo:name "Healthy eating" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Healthy eating should be encouraged without additional calories or carbohydrates for women who are bottle feeding" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/lifestyleIntervention/lifestyle-modifications> a dmo:LifestyleIntervention ;
    rdfs:label "Lifestyle modifications" ;
    dmo:activityTarget "Women with gestational diabetes have a ten times increased risk of developing type 2 diabetes within 5 years" ;
    dmo:expectedBenefit "Women with GDM are eligible for referral to the National Diabetes Prevention Programme (NDPP) regardless of baseline glycaemia (provided that type 2 diabetes has been excluded)" ;
    dmo:interventionCode "lifestyle-modifications" ;
    dmo:interventionType "SelfManagementEd" ;
    dmo:name "Lifestyle modifications" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Lifestyle modifications. Women with gestational diabetes have a ten times increased risk of developing type 2 diabetes within 5 years (23). Women with GDM are eligible for referral to the National Diabetes Prevention Programme (NDPP) regardless of baseline glycaemia (provided that type 2 diabetes has been excluded)" ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/jbds_12_managing_diabetes_and_hyperglycaemia_during_labour_and_birth_with_qr_code_february_2023> .

